                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 902,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1116,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Takes a signal that swings back and forth and separates it into how big the swing is at each moment - the envelope - and where it is in its cycle - the phase - plus how fast that phase is turning: the instantaneous frequency. It uses the Hilbert transform to build the signal's \"analytic\" partner, a wave a quarter cycle behind it.\n\nPhase is the part that is hard to get otherwise: it says at every sample whether a repetitive movement is at the top of its swing, on the way down, at the bottom - so two performers' phases can be compared for synchrony, a gait can be followed through its cycle, or sound can be locked to the cycle of a movement. The envelope follows the size of the swing without the ripples of the swing itself.\n\nIt works best on a signal with one main rhythm; band-pass it first if there are several. Every row of a stack (every joint of a buffer, with dim 0 for frames x joints) is analysed at once, on the input's device. It is scipy.hilbert, for tensors.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.hilbert <sample rate>\nDefault: sample rate 60.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe signal (a list or tensor). Every input is analysed and sent.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second, for the frequency outlet.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way time runs: -1 along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nremove mean:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), the average level is taken away first, so the phase measures the swing about the middle.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nenvelope:\nThe size of the swing at each sample.\n\nphase:\nWhere in its cycle each sample is, in degrees from -180 to 180. 0 is the top of the swing ... -90 rising through the middle, 90 falling.\n\nfrequency:\nHow fast the phase turns, in cycles per second. Sent first, then phase, then envelope.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_swell subpatcher makes a wave at 2 cycles a second that grows from 0.2 to 1 across 10 seconds (left). Its envelope (middle) is a smooth ramp from 0.2 to 1, without the wiggles; its phase (right) is a sawtooth climbing from -180 to 180 twice a second - 20 teeth in the 10 seconds - the position in each cycle.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.hilbert is the numpy version. envelope and speech_envelope follow loudness in a live stream. t.stft shows every frequency at every moment rather than one rhythm's phase.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1100,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Takes a signal that swings back and forth and separates it into how big the swing is at each moment - the envelope - and where it is in its cycle - the phase - plus how fast that phase is turning: the instantaneous frequency. It uses the Hilbert transform to build the signal's \"analytic\" partner, a wave a quarter cycle behind it.\n\nPhase is the part that is hard to get otherwise: it says at every sample whether a repetitive movement is at the top of its swing, on the way down, at the bottom - so two performers' phases can be compared for synchrony, a gait can be followed through its cycle, or sound can be locked to the cycle of a movement. The envelope follows the size of the swing without the ripples of the swing itself.\n\nIt works best on a signal with one main rhythm; band-pass it first if there are several. Every row of a stack (every joint of a buffer, with dim 0 for frames x joints) is analysed at once, on the input's device. It is scipy.hilbert, for tensors.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.hilbert <sample rate>\nDefault: sample rate 60.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe signal (a list or tensor). Every input is analysed and sent.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second, for the frequency outlet.\n\ndim (int box, also an inlet):\nWhich way time runs: -1 along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nremove mean:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), the average level is taken away first, so the phase measures the swing about the middle.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nenvelope:\nThe size of the swing at each sample.\n\nphase:\nWhere in its cycle each sample is, in degrees from -180 to 180. 0 is the top of the swing ... -90 rising through the middle, 90 falling.\n\nfrequency:\nHow fast the phase turns, in cycles per second. Sent first, then phase, then envelope.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe make_swell subpatcher makes a wave at 2 cycles a second that grows from 0.2 to 1 across 10 seconds (left). Its envelope (middle) is a smooth ramp from 0.2 to 1, without the wiggles; its phase (right) is a sawtooth climbing from -180 to 180 twice a second - 20 teeth in the 10 seconds - the position in each cycle.\n\nRELATED:\nscipy.hilbert is the numpy version. envelope and speech_envelope follow loudness in a live stream. t.stft shows every frequency at every moment rather than one rhythm's phase."
                },
